"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,711\nThe Learning Brain. Series 6: Neuromyths\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,333\nEpisode 2\n\n3\n00:00:07,413 --> 00:00:09,251\nLearning styles\n\n4\n00:00:12,051 --> 00:00:13,194\nVisual learner.\n\n5\n00:00:13,337 --> 00:00:14,629\nAuditory learner.\n\n6\n00:00:14,841 --> 00:00:16,122\nKinesthetic learner.\n\n7\n00:00:16,346 --> 00:00:19,831\nIt may be the most widely believed idea in education.\n\n8\n00:00:20,152 --> 00:00:21,098\nIs it true?\n\n9\n00:00:21,268 --> 00:00:22,738\nThe claim is specific.\n\n10\n00:00:23,173 --> 00:00:26,777\nStudents learn better when teaching matches their preferred style.\n\n11\n00:00:27,124 --> 00:00:30,123\nResearchers call this the meshing hypothesis.\n\n12\n00:00:30,293 --> 00:00:32,826\nTo test it, you need a particular experiment.\n\n13\n00:00:33,255 --> 00:00:34,941\nSort students by style.\n\n14\n00:00:35,279 --> 00:00:37,552\nTeach each group in both formats.\n\n15\n00:00:37,807 --> 00:00:40,004\nThen give everyone the same test.\n\n16\n00:00:40,434 --> 00:00:45,338\nMeshing predicts a crossover: visual learners do best with visual teaching,\n\n17\n00:00:45,375 --> 00:00:48,345\nand auditory learners with auditory teaching.\n\n18\n00:00:48,515 --> 00:00:53,167\nIn 2008, Harold Pashler and colleagues reviewed the research.\n\n19\n00:00:53,538 --> 00:00:55,795\nVery few studies used that design.\n\n20\n00:00:56,217 --> 00:00:59,672\nThose that did found virtually no evidence for meshing,\n\n21\n00:00:59,870 --> 00:01:01,456\nand several contradicted it.\n\n22\n00:01:01,945 --> 00:01:04,829\nLater reviews have reached the same conclusion.\n\n23\n00:01:04,999 --> 00:01:06,871\nYet the idea is everywhere.\n\n24\n00:01:07,408 --> 00:01:08,843\nIn one British survey,\n\n25\n00:01:08,988 --> 00:01:13,764\nseventy six percent of educators said they used learning styles in their practice.\n\n26\n00:01:14,124 --> 00:01:17,211\nAnd there is a thriving industry of style tests,\n\n27\n00:01:17,396 --> 00:01:19,229\nguidebooks and workshops.\n\n28\n00:01:19,399 --> 00:01:20,690\nThe grain of truth: people really do have preferences,\n\n29\n00:01:20,976 --> 00:01:24,315\nand differ in abilities.\n\n30\n00:01:24,664 --> 00:01:27,527\nBut the best format depends mostly on the content.\n\n31\n00:01:27,968 --> 00:01:29,980\nA map is visual for everyone.\n\n32\n00:01:30,374 --> 00:01:32,830\nA melody is auditory for everyone.\n\n33\n00:01:33,215 --> 00:01:37,661\nAnd a format that feels easier is not always the one that teaches more,\n\n34\n00:01:37,752 --> 00:01:39,617\nas we saw in Series four.\n\n35\n00:01:39,787 --> 00:01:40,948\nDo this instead.\n\n36\n00:01:41,342 --> 00:01:43,511\nChoose the format that fits the content,\n\n37\n00:01:43,531 --> 00:01:46,247\nand combine words with pictures where it helps.\n\n38\n00:01:46,599 --> 00:01:49,093\nUse methods that work for nearly everyone,\n\n39\n00:01:49,113 --> 00:01:51,390\nlike retrieval practice and spacing.\n\n40\n00:01:51,750 --> 00:01:54,541\nAnd drop style inventories from your course.\n\n41\n00:01:54,983 --> 00:01:59,084\nOne tip to share with students: pick study methods by what works,\n\n42\n00:01:59,153 --> 00:02:00,469\nnot by your style.\n\n43\n00:02:00,639 --> 00:02:01,970\nStudents are different.\n\n44\n00:02:02,406 --> 00:02:05,953\nBut the strategies that help them learn are mostly the same.\n\n"